Run a tree cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --tree --time 60
 --nodes N sets the tree node budget (default 10,000). --json FILE writesvalidated incumbent routes, bounds, statuses, and work counters. Use--no-enumeration to disable both reduced-cost fixing and enumeration. A complete Rust entry point, requiring clp: use baldes::{
    master::{root::RootProblem, tree::{self, TreeOptions}},
    vrptw::{Instance, Rounding},
};
use std::time::Duration;

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let graph = instance.graph(8, Rounding::None)?;
    let problem = RootProblem::set_partitioning(graph, instance.vehicles as f64)?;
    let mut options = TreeOptions::default();
    options.time_limit = Duration::from_secs(60);
    options.root.cg.multiplicity = (instance.customers.len() - 1) as f64;
    let result = tree::solve(problem, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: incumbent={}, bound={}",
        result.status, result.incumbent, result.bound);
    Ok(())
}
 The CLI tree uses Instance::vehicles as its fleet bound. Root-only CLI andInstance::solve_root use the customer count instead. A custom Rust callerchooses the bound in RootProblem::set_partitioning and configures thecolumn-generation multiplicity separately.

Start with capacity and rank-one cuts cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 25 --capacity-cuts --rank1 --time 60
 A complete Rust example: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, vrptw::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(25)?;
    let rounding = Rounding::None;
    let capacity = instance.capacity_cut_config(rounding, false);
    let options = RootOptions::default()
        .with_capacity_cuts(capacity)
        .with_rank_one_cuts();
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, rounding, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Use capacity_cut_config(rounding, true) to attach the instance's ready/duewindows, service times, and travel matrix for Kohl separation. The CLI/Python2-path switch is for VRPTW; CVRP inputs do not supply time windows. Capacity/Kohl separation has priority 3 at the root and 1 below it. It shares apersistent subset pool. Basic cut rows may be retired by cleanup; reported cutcounts can therefore exceed the number of rows still active.

Controls and API Build and enable the optional tree path: cargo run --features clp,highs --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --tree --enumerated-mip
 TreeOptions::enumerated_mip = Some(MipOptions::default()) enables the samepath in Rust. It is disabled by default to preserve the existing tree schedule.A build without highs rejects this request explicitly. Disabling enumerationleaves no complete universe to send to the MIP solver. MipOptions defaults to 2,000,000 nodes, 360,000 seconds, and no objectivecutoff. The tree additionally caps time at 98% of its remaining global budget.These budgets are separate from the restricted-column heuristic'sTreeOptions::mip_node_limit / mip_time_limit. For a generic integer model, the highs feature alone is sufficient. Thiscomplete example builds a two-variable binary master: use baldes::master::{
    solvers::{highs::{self, HighsMaster}, mip::MipOptions},
    LpMaster, RowSense,
};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut master = HighsMaster::new()?;
    let row = master.add_row(RowSense::GreaterEqual, 1.5, &[])?;
    for _ in 0..2 {
        let col = master.add_column(1.0, &[(row, 1.0)])?;
        master.set_upper(col, 1.0)?;
    }
    let result = highs::solve_integer(&master, &[0, 1], &MipOptions::default())?;
    println!("{:?}: {:?}", result.status, result.solution);
    Ok(())
}
 This copies every current physical row, coefficient, cost, and upper boundthrough LpMaster. Columns have lower bound zero in this interface. Unlistedcolumns remain continuous. Integer values and all row activities are checkedbefore returning a feasible witness. Coefficients small enough for HiGHS todiscard are rejected explicitly instead of silently changing the matrix. For a routing snapshot, NodeSnapshot::solve_enumerated_mip prepares the fullroute master and applies the end-of-node route-count gate. It returns Noneif enumeration is incomplete or over the limit. This snapshot API requiresclp,highs; the generic highs::solve_integer API needs only highs.

Examples and verification Rust exposes metadata independently of constructing a graph: use baldes::{core::options::Options, pricing::Solver};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    println!("{:?}", Options::support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")?);
    let mut solver = Solver::new(3, 1)?;
    solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)?;
    Ok(())
}
 Python exposes the same support query and runtime setter: from pybaldes import Solver

names = Solver.option_names()
implemented, scope = Solver.option_support("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy")
solver = Solver(3, 1)
solver.set_option("BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy", 1.0)
 CLI overrides are repeatable: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 \
  --option BALDESlabelDominanceStrategy=1 --time 60
 OPTION_TABLE is the source of truth for names,types, defaults, and feature-dependent support. It lists only controls supportedin the current build. Keep this document synchronized when adding a consumer.Bool values are numeric zero/one; integer optionsrequire integral values. Live setters also validate operation-specific modesand limits before changing solver state. Option tests cover canonical discovery in Rust/C,input-alias compatibility, failure atomicity, dominance synchronization, andcapacity overridesin fresh/inherited nodes. Run: cargo test --features clp,highs --test options
cargo test --features clp --test options --test cli
 See Build and validation for the full suite. Separate featurebuilds matter because MIP threshold support changes with the enabled backends.

Configure or disable A complete Rust example, requiring clp: use baldes::{master::root::RootOptions, vrptw::{Instance, Rounding}};

fn main() -> baldes::Result<()> {
    let mut instance = Instance::read("instances/R101.txt")?;
    instance.truncate(10)?;
    let mut options = RootOptions::default();
    options.cg.smoothing.initial_alpha = 0.5;
    options.cg.smoothing.beta_factor = 0.0;
    options.cg.smoothing.max_updates = 10;
    let result = instance.solve_root(8, Rounding::None, options)?;
    println!("{:?}: root LP={}", result.master.status, result.master.lp);
    Ok(())
}
 Set options.cg.stabilize = false to disable it in Rust. The CLI exposes: cargo run --release --features clp --bin baldes -- \
  vrptw instances/R101.txt --customers 10 --no-stabilization
 Python exposes the enable/disable switch through the public root API: import pybaldes

result = pybaldes.solve_root(
    "instances/R101.txt", customers=10, stabilize=False,
)
print(result.status, result.lp)
 The Python root API does not expose the individual smoothing parameters.
